WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.367
-Our next guest is an Emmy-
nominated comedian and actress

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:04.901
you know from her
Netflix comedy specials

00:00:04.901 --> 00:00:07.133
and shows like "Big Mouth,"
"Tuca &amp; Bertie,"

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:08.634
"and Human Resources."

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:11.667
She stars in "Beef," which
premieres on Netflix April 6th.

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:12.801
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:15.200
-Come on. Talk to me.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.167
-Okay, well, um,
you know, after the meeting,

00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:25.067
I was sitting in the parking lot
thinking about how many meetings

00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:28.200
and calls I've had to do
for the last two years.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.133
Still, somehow
there's no offer coming.

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.667
And it just really
got to me, you know?

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:35.868
So then I started driving,
and then there was this guy --

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:37.968
-Hey, hey.
Before you spiral...

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:40.701
I'm gonna have you stop
right there.

00:00:40.701 --> 00:00:42.234
Take a deep breath.

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:45.634
[ Inhales deeply ]
Pause.

00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:49.901
You got to start focusing
on the positive. Okay?

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:52.067
You know, maybe
we should start doing

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:54.033
the gratitude journals again.

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:55.901
-Please welcome back
to the show Ali Wong,

00:00:55.901 --> 00:00:57.367
everybody!

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:59.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:07.234
♪♪

00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:14.934
♪♪

00:01:14.934 --> 00:01:17.334
Welcome back, Ali.
-Thanks for having me.

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:19.701
-I'm so thrilled to have you.
I know you were --

00:01:19.701 --> 00:01:21.968
Early days, you were in New York
working on standup.

00:01:21.968 --> 00:01:24.534
-I was -- I would do --
When I lived here, my God --

00:01:24.534 --> 00:01:27.801
and hustling, I would do up
to sometimes 13 sets a night.

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:29.334
-That is insane to me.

00:01:29.334 --> 00:01:31.100
Bouncing all around the city?

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.033
-Bouncing all around the city.
Sometimes to Brooklyn,

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.934
back to, like,
the Upper East Side,

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:39.501
to, like, Bed-Stuy,
to the Lower East Side.

00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:42.801
And it was all for no money.
Sometimes three-minute sets.

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:44.334
-And, now, when you were
doing the same --

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:46.167
I'm assuming you're doing
the same three minutes

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:49.100
13 different times,
13 different audiences.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.234
-Yeah.
-Would they just be

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:52.767
wildly different
as far as what worked where?

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:55.167
-Yeah, but I wasn't really
paying attention to that.

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:58.167
Like, I never paid attention
much to the audiences

00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:00.734
because they were so crappy.

00:02:00.734 --> 00:02:02.901
And a lot of the times
it was like,

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:06.534
you know, like,
random crazy people

00:02:06.534 --> 00:02:08.434
jerking off into their eggs.
-Yeah.

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:10.133
-So it was like --
Who did not know

00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:12.400
there was a comedy show
going on.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.200
Oh, am I -- am I allowed
to say that about the eggs?

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.901
Oh, well. It's too late now.

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:18.167
-Well, you know what?

00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:19.901
We are gonna get you
to ADR later --

00:02:19.901 --> 00:02:22.501
"Putting salt on their..."

00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:24.601
Real quick.
So I want to ask, though.

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:26.767
But you're getting no money,
and it doesn't seem like

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:28.167
you're getting helpful feedback.

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:30.100
So what were you
getting out of it?

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.667
-Well, it's more like
I have to pay attention

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:34.968
when I say it out loud
to the energy.

00:02:34.968 --> 00:02:37.634
And, like, if I --
if I have confidence,

00:02:37.634 --> 00:02:39.834
even if I bomb, like,
it's still funny.

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:41.934
That's really
valuable information.

00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:44.033
You know, like if I still
feel like, okay,

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:46.000
even though this didn't
get much laughs,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.834
like if I still feel
attached to it,

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.234
that tells me something,
you know?

00:02:50.234 --> 00:02:53.567
-That -- I should also note
you make it look very easy,

00:02:53.567 --> 00:02:56.367
your specials, and I think
it's a very nice thing for you

00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:58.901
to admit how hard you have to
work to make it look so easy.

00:02:58.901 --> 00:03:02.367
-Yeah, it was like it was --
And I still eat it a lot.

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:04.534
Like, when I go up at places,

00:03:04.534 --> 00:03:06.501
people are always so excited
to see me,

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:09.501
and then I sort of talk
in this very, like, hushed --

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:12.300
Like, I don't put a lot
of performance into it.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.067
And I talk in a sort of
monotone voice,

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:16.100
and then I can see
people's, like, face --

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.501
They're really excited,
and then it starts to fall,

00:03:18.501 --> 00:03:21.334
and they're like,
"Oh, my God. She's lost it."

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:24.534
And then -- But, again,
it's really about the writing.

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:26.467
After a while,
it would get to the point

00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:28.267
where no matter where I went,

00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:31.200
those three minutes
would always still get laughs.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.133
-That's such
a fascinating process.

00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:34.634
-Yeah.
-I'm so happy

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:36.534
you put yourself through it.

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:38.167
So then, though, I didn't
realize you moved back to L.A.

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:40.367
You're doing all this work
on a comedy career,

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.467
and then you go to L.A.
-Well, I went to L.A.

00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:44.801
actually to play
Christian Slater's assistant

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:46.634
in this sitcom,
and then I moved back

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:50.467
to New York for like a year
to be on this medical drama.

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:52.801
-Oh. So that was in New York.
-That was in New York.

00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:56.434
I played
an agoraphobic radiologist

00:03:56.434 --> 00:03:58.667
who had a love arc
with a plumber.

00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:00.234
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah.

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:03.601
-You would be perfect for that.

00:04:03.601 --> 00:04:06.167
-And then, like, that was
the first dramatic role

00:04:06.167 --> 00:04:07.400
I ever had to do, kind of,

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.000
because I had --
It wasn't very funny,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.734
but I was always
trying to be funny,

00:04:12.734 --> 00:04:15.567
and the director was like,
"Stop trying to make

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:17.467
'occipital lobe' funny!

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:20.133
Just say what's going on
in the brain!"

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:25.501
And then I -- The show
also had Vanessa Redgrave.

00:04:25.501 --> 00:04:27.567
Sure. A legend.
-A legend.

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:31.234
And then I remember overhearing
one of the directors talk about

00:04:31.234 --> 00:04:33.100
directing Vanessa Redgrave,

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.033
and he was like,
"Oh, when she's on camera,

00:04:35.033 --> 00:04:37.601
it's like directing
a Rolls-Royce."

00:04:37.601 --> 00:04:38.934
And then I just walked away

00:04:38.934 --> 00:04:40.901
because I knew
if someone asked him,

00:04:40.901 --> 00:04:43.734
"What is it like to direct Ali?"
he would have said,

00:04:43.734 --> 00:04:47.567
"It's like directing
a Toyota Yaris."

00:04:47.567 --> 00:04:50.367
So I just left
because I was like,

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:51.901
"I don't want my feelings hurt.'
-I'm gonna use

00:04:51.901 --> 00:04:54.000
the mention of cars
to segue very smoothly

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.901
to this wonderful
new show, "Beef."

00:04:55.901 --> 00:04:58.400
This is about two people
who have a road-rage incident

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.801
and then become obsessed
with each other.

00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:02.601
Excuse me.
Your costar, Steven Yeun.

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:04.100
He's wonderful.
-He's amazing.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:07.000
-And this is a -- I mean,
there's very funny parts of it.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.834
It's also very dramatic
at times.

00:05:08.834 --> 00:05:10.467
-Yeah.
-And it's also very different.

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:12.667
We were talking backstage.
It seems very unique to me.

00:05:12.667 --> 00:05:14.734
Did you enjoy it from the first
time you laid eyes on it?

00:05:14.734 --> 00:05:16.501
-So when, like, the creator,

00:05:16.501 --> 00:05:18.734
Lee Sung Jin,
came to me with Steven,

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:20.834
I just really wanted to work
with those guys.

00:05:20.834 --> 00:05:23.133
That was, like, the biggest
priority. That was the biggest.

00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:24.834
Like, it was like,
"You had me at hello."

00:05:24.834 --> 00:05:26.701
Because I always wanted
to work with them.

00:05:26.701 --> 00:05:28.801
And then after they told me
about the concept,

00:05:28.801 --> 00:05:30.667
I was so excited,
and then when I read the pilot,

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:34.133
I just read it on my toilet
in one sitting,

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:36.334
and I had -- and my kids
kept on knocking on the door,

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:38.501
and I was like, "Hold on,
hold on, hold on, hold on!"

00:05:38.501 --> 00:05:40.267
And I kept on, like,
scrolling through the iPad.

00:05:40.267 --> 00:05:42.067
And then I had that, like,
signature ring

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:43.601
around my butt afterwards,

00:05:43.601 --> 00:05:47.067
and I was like, "Oh, this
is a good project. Yeah."

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:49.367
-You called your agent and
you're like, "I got the ring."

00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:51.300
-Yeah, yeah. "I got the ring."

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.534
-And you --
Did you first meet Steven

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:55.567
when he came
to see you do stand-up?

00:05:55.567 --> 00:05:56.968
-Yeah. He was so sweet.

00:05:56.968 --> 00:05:59.567
So, when my first stand-up
special, "Baby Cobra,"

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:01.167
came out...
-Fantastic.

00:06:01.167 --> 00:06:03.734
-Oh, thanks.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:03.734 --> 00:06:07.634
He sent a message to me on
Twitter about how great it was,

00:06:07.634 --> 00:06:10.133
and he encouraged all of
his followers to watch it.

00:06:10.133 --> 00:06:14.534
And then he came, and I think
he even paid money to --

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:16.033
for him
and a bunch of his friends

00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:19.767
to come see me
at the Ace in downtown L.A.

00:06:19.767 --> 00:06:22.834
-Did you know he was coming?
-I think I knew he was coming.

00:06:22.834 --> 00:06:24.767
And then he came backstage
afterwards.

00:06:24.767 --> 00:06:27.100
And then one of his friends
kept on taking pictures of me,

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.067
and I was like,
"Hey, man, what are you doing?!"

00:06:29.067 --> 00:06:31.501
And Steven was like,
"This is my friend.

00:06:31.501 --> 00:06:33.467
He's a portrait artist."

00:06:33.467 --> 00:06:36.234
And that guy we ended up casting
as the husband that you see

00:06:36.234 --> 00:06:38.000
in the clip.
-That was in the clip.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.334
-Yeah.
-So there you go.

00:06:39.334 --> 00:06:41.601
-He plays an artist
in the show, and then he's --

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:44.067
he's a real-life, incredible,
incredible painter, yeah.

00:06:44.067 --> 00:06:46.300
-Well, that's fantastic.
It's really a great show.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.000
And it's so lovely
to have you here.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.434
Thanks for being back with us.
-Thanks so much for having me.

00:06:50.434 --> 00:06:53.434
-Ali Wong, everybody. "Beef"
premieres on Netflix April 6th.

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:55.634
We'll be right back with music
from The Hold Steady.

